research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-closed-unit-interval-has-one-compatible-uniformity.md-414-3.1 Substituting claim 2 into the definition of open in the metric topology gives claim 3: $U$ is open exactly when every $x \in U$ admits $r > 0$ with $(x-r,x+r) = B(x,r) \subseteq U$. [step 2.2]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-closed-unit-interval-has-one-compatible-uniformity.md-415-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-closed-unit-interval-has-one-compatible-uniformity.md-416-4.1 Since $x_0$ and $r$ were arbitrary, step 2.3 exhibits for every ball $B(x_0,r)$ a real not in it, so no ball contains $\mathbb{R}$; hence $\mathbb{R}$ is not a bounded subset of itself and $\operatorname{diam}(\mathbb{R})$ is not defined, which is claim 4. [step 2.1, step 2.3] ∎
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-closed-unit-interval-has-one-compatible-uniformity.md-417-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-closed-unit-interval-has-one-compatible-uniformity.md-418-## Remarks
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-closed-unit-interval-has-one-compatible-uniformity.md-419-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-closed-unit-interval-has-one-compatible-uniformity.md-420-- **This is the metric every later ceiling rests on.** Every real-line example on the companion page, and every subspace of $\mathbb{R}$ used there, takes its metric from $d_{\mathbb{R}}$ through the subspace construction of [[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]].
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-closed-unit-interval-has-one-compatible-uniformity.md-421-- **Unboundedness needs no Archimedean input, and no completeness either.** No ordered field is bounded under $d(u,v) = |u-v|$, and the reason is a single element rather than any cofinality property: given a centre $c$ and a radius $r > 0$, the element $c + r + 1$ satisfies $d(c,\, c + r + 1) = |{-(r+1)}| = r + 1 > r$, because $1 > 0$ and $r + 1 > 0$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]], [[cor-of-one-positive]], [[lem-of-add-order]]); so it lies outside $B(c,r)$ and no ball contains the field. Step 1.6 above chooses its witness with [[thm-of-archimedean]] instead, which is a convenience and not a necessity: it delivers a witness that is a canonical natural, and claim 4 needs no such thing. Claim 4 therefore holds verbatim in every ordered field with this $d$, Archimedean or not. Note also that a radius is an element of $\mathbb{R}$, so "a ball of infinite radius" is not something that can be written here.
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-closed-unit-interval-has-one-compatible-uniformity.md-422-- **The claim that $\operatorname{diam}(\mathbb{R})$ is "not defined" is a claim about the conventions of this development** ([[rem-sup-conventions]], [[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]): suprema here are real numbers and the extended real line, which is introduced on a later page, is not used for them, so an unbounded set has no diameter at all rather than a diameter $+\infty$.
