research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-24-{"id":"lem-minimal-cauchy-filter-space-is-complete","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.boletin.math.org.mx/pdf/2/34/BSMM%282%29.34.11-21.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources construct the completion from minimal Cauchy filters; completeness of that space is part of the construction. The item isolates and expands that component.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-25-{"id":"lem-point-filters-are-dense-in-the-minimal-cauchy-filter-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.boletin.math.org.mx/pdf/2/34/BSMM%282%29.34.11-21.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki 6.1.6-6.2.4 gives the point-neighborhood-filter map, uniform continuity, and density. The item packages these conclusions; A3 proposes the member-contains-the-point clause actually used later.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-26-{"id":"thm-hausdorff-completion-of-a-uniform-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Complete_uniform_space"],"rationale":"The sources state existence of a dense complete Hausdorff completion; Wodzicki also identifies when the canonical map reflects the original uniformity. The item packages the reflection and embedding criterion.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-27-{"id":"thm-uniform-completion-universal-property-and-uniqueness","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space"],"rationale":"Wodzicki 6.2.3 states the same unique extension of every uniformly continuous map into a complete Hausdorff uniform space. Uniqueness of completions is its exact categorical consequence.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-28-{"id":"def-totally-bounded-uniform-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Encyclopedia of Mathematics defines precompactness by finite uniform-cover refinement, equivalently finitely many entourage balls. The item adopts the standard name totally bounded and the entourage formulation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-29-{"id":"lem-ultrafilters-on-totally-bounded-uniform-spaces-are-cauchy","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. This is the standard ultrafilter characterization behind precompactness, and the finite-cover proof is elementary, but the located Encyclopedia source states only the surrounding completion/precompactness theorem rather than this exact all-ultrafilter proposition. It is established, not novel. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: The finite entourage-ball cover plus ultrafilter primeness argument is the standard proof that every ultrafilter on a totally bounded uniform space is Cauchy. This familiar lemma is established mathematics extracted from the classical compactness equivalence.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-30-{"id":"lem-compact-uniform-spaces-are-complete","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Complete_uniform_space"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Compact uniform spaces are standardly complete, and the item gives the usual cluster-point proof, but the located Encyclopedia discussion has already imposed separatedness while this item correctly allows nonseparated uniformities. The broader statement is established but not an exact source match. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Compact uniform spaces are classically complete via the cluster-point argument for Cauchy filters. Separation is needed for uniqueness, not for existence of a limit, so the nonseparated local convention is a standard broadening rather than a novel result.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-31-{"id":"lem-compact-uniform-spaces-are-totally-bounded","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Compact uniform spaces are standardly totally bounded, with the usual finite entourage-ball subcover proof. The located Encyclopedia statement sits under a separated-uniformity convention, while this item is broader, so the hypothesis delta precludes exact-source treatment. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: The finite-subcover proof from entourage-ball neighbourhoods is the standard theorem that compact uniform spaces are totally bounded. The argument does not require separatedness, making the local convention delta established and mathematically routine.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-32-{"id":"thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-uniform-spaces-are-compact","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Complete-plus-precompact implies compact is standard, but the item explicitly assumes only the ultrafilter lemma, whereas the source works classically. The local proof verifies that weaker assumption; its extra full-Choice citation is an A3 defect. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Complete plus totally bounded implies compact is a classical uniform-space theorem. The proof through convergence of ultrafilters uses precisely the ultrafilter lemma; making that weak-choice hypothesis explicit is a standard foundational calibration, not invention.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-33-{"id":"cor-uniform-compactness-equivalence","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Compact iff complete and totally bounded is standard, but the item records the precise ultrafilter-lemma assumption for the reverse implication. No located source states that same foundational calibration. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: The compactness equivalence is the classical combination of compact-implies-complete, compact-implies-totally-bounded, and the converse. Its explicit ultrafilter-lemma scope is a faithful foundational restatement of established mathematics.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-34-{"id":"lem-finite-star-refinement-for-compact-hausdorff-spaces","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"The compact-Hausdorff uniformization proofs in the sources construct finite open refinements with the required star control. The item extracts that standard construction as a named lemma.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-35-{"id":"thm-compact-hausdorff-open-cover-uniformity","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources construct the unique compatible uniformity on a compact Hausdorff space from diagonal neighborhoods and open covers. The item uses the equivalent cover formulation. Its final entourage recovery fails for the locally admitted empty compact space.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl:36:{"id":"thm-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-unique-compatible-uniformity","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"Vienna Theorem 9.4.14 and Wodzicki Corollary 4.8 give uniqueness, with Vienna also giving existence, under the standard nonempty convention. The library admits empty compact Hausdorff space but no uniformity on it, so the exactly-one claim needs nonemptiness.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-37-{"id":"cor-compact-domain-maps-are-uniformly-continuous","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki Theorem 4.7 and BIU state the compact-domain theorem for the natural compatible uniformity. The library convention requires a nonempty domain, and the proof needs the cover dictionary cited explicitly.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-38-{"id":"def-countably-based-uniformity","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources define a uniform base by cofinal refinement and countable base exactly as the item does.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-39-{"id":"lem-countable-uniform-base-normalisation","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Decreasing symmetric bases with triple-composite control are the standard metrization normalization, but the item emphasizes its finite-intersection recursion works in ZF. No located source states this exact choice-free proposition. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Normalizing a countable entourage base to a decreasing symmetric base with triple-composite control is the standard first step in uniform-space metrization. Choosing least indices in a fixed enumeration makes the usual construction choice-free, so the ZF form remains established mathematics.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-40-{"id":"thm-countably-based-uniformity-is-pseudometrizable","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give pseudometrizability of countably based uniformities and the separated criterion for the pseudometric to distinguish points. The item packages both; its metric criterion needs a direct metric-definition citation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-41-{"id":"def-uniformizable-space","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki and Vienna define uniformizable as having a compatible uniformity; the item additionally names the standard separated variant without altering either definition.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-42-{"id":"lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-regular","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources state the regularity consequence of a compatible uniformity with the same non-T1 convention. The proof is the standard entourage-shrinking argument.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-43-{"id":"lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-completely-regular","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Literature proves uniformizable spaces completely regular via pseudometrics, but the item makes DC explicit as the construction principle. No source with that same foundational hypothesis surfaced. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: The pseudometric-separation proof that uniformizable spaces are completely regular is classical. DC is exposed only to build the normal entourage sequence used by that proof, so the local theorem is an established choice-calibrated version.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-44-{"id":"lem-completely-regular-topologies-come-from-continuous-pseudometrics","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"Vienna Theorem 9.4.15 and BIU use continuous functions p_f(x,y)=|f(x)-f(y)| to recover the topology. The item makes the gauge explicit; its empty-carrier and triangle-inequality citation defects are escalated.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-45-{"id":"thm-uniformizable-iff-completely-regular","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The uniformization theorem is standard, but the biconditional asserts DC suffices and the local definition admits empty completely regular space while proper filters exclude it. No source matches both conventions and hypothesis. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Uniformizability iff complete regularity under the separation-free convention is a classical theorem. The current nonempty hypothesis resolves the library's proper-filter edge case, and the explicit DC hypothesis records the standard construction's choice cost rather than adding new content.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-46-{"id":"cor-separated-uniformizable-iff-tychonoff","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The separated uniformization theorem is standard, but the item exposes DC and the library Tychonoff convention. Empty carrier is currently a counterexample, and T1/Hausdorff bridge citations are incomplete. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Separated-uniformizable iff Tychonoff is the classical separated form of the uniformization theorem. The repaired nonempty domain, explicit DC, and locally stated T1 convention are recoverable convention and hypothesis adjustments to that standard result.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-47-{"id":"def-topological-group","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"The BIU notes define a topological group by continuity of multiplication and inversion, exactly matching the item.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-48-{"id":"lem-topological-group-translations-and-inversion","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"The BIU notes state that left and right shifts and inversion are homeomorphisms. The proof expands continuity and inverse formulas.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
